WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:02.700
Come on down, Miami open for business.

00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.000
Bubbling of activity, and excitement, and optimism. 

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.763
And not just because of the taxes, but because of the culture of innovation.

00:00:15.760 --> 00:00:18.120
When the Venture
Capitalist Delian Asparouhov

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:19.730
suggested on Twitter last December

00:00:19.730 --> 00:00:21.280
that the tech industry should migrate

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:22.850
from Silicon Valley to Miami,

00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:24.321
Mayor Francis Forest responded,

00:00:24.321 --> 00:00:25.600
"How can I help?"

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.747
He also set up a billboard
in San Francisco.

00:00:27.747 --> 00:00:30.120
"Thinking of moving to Miami? DM Me."

00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:32.650
Suarez’s bold, roll-out-the-red-carpet approach

00:00:32.650 --> 00:00:34.580
to luring away Silicon
Valley's tech elites

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:35.790
has gotten so much attention

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.560
in part, because of how it contrasts

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.250
with that of California's
ultra-left political class.

00:00:40.250 --> 00:00:42.590
Take San Diego Assemblywoman Lorena Gonzalez,

00:00:42.590 --> 00:00:45.330
who's best known for her failed effort to stop Uber and Lyft

00:00:45.330 --> 00:00:47.210
from using contract labor in California,

00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:48.700
to benefit labor unions.

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:50.880
Gonzalez tweeted, "Fuck Elon Musk"

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:52.007
back in May of 2020.

00:00:52.007 --> 00:00:54.680
"Message received." replied Musk.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.820
In Miami, Mayor Suarez has embraced Musk's idea

00:00:56.820 --> 00:00:58.510
of building a 30 million dollar tunnel

00:00:58.510 --> 00:01:00.750
for electric vehicles to ease congestion.

00:01:00.750 --> 00:01:04.120
We have a unique opportunity
to create a signature project

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.803
not just for Miami, but for the world.

00:01:11.776 --> 00:01:12.940
Suarez's publicity stunts,

00:01:12.940 --> 00:01:16.000
including fashioning himself
an avid Bitcoin enthusiast,

00:01:16.000 --> 00:01:18.210
have no doubt contributed
to the city's momentum,

00:01:18.210 --> 00:01:20.960
but Miami's greatest advantage
isn't the VC heavyweights

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:22.670
buying mansions on the waterfront.

00:01:22.670 --> 00:01:25.100
It's the Latino immigrants,
who don't garner headlines,

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:27.230
or donate much to political campaigns,

00:01:27.230 --> 00:01:28.410
but have brought with them grit,

00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:31.050
and talent largely wasted
in the socialist countries

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:32.040
from which they fled.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:33.790
Not to mention their cultural aversion

00:01:33.790 --> 00:01:35.160
to big government liberalism,

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:37.960
and the woke ideology now
prevalent in the Bay Area.

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:40.490
Miami's ascendance in
the 21st Century hinges

00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:42.450
on whether it can continue
to fulfill its role

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:44.240
as a greatest city in Latin America,

00:01:44.240 --> 00:01:47.420
that just happens to be
located in the United States.

00:01:47.420 --> 00:01:48.820
Nearly half a million Cubans

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.010
have come to settle in Miami Dade County,

00:01:51.010 --> 00:01:54.630
and these Cubans seem to
be in Dade County to stay.

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:55.764
Suarez himself is a scion

00:01:55.764 --> 00:01:58.620
of a Cuban American political
dynasty in South Florida.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.980
His father, Javier, became
Miami's first Cuban-born mayor,

00:02:01.980 --> 00:02:03.090
in 1985.

00:02:03.090 --> 00:02:05.130
The Suarez clan probably
wouldn't be here at all,

00:02:05.130 --> 00:02:06.650
if it weren't for the communist dictator

00:02:06.650 --> 00:02:07.483
Fidel Castro,

00:02:07.483 --> 00:02:08.520
who deserves the most credit

00:02:08.520 --> 00:02:10.473
for Miami becoming a world-class city.

00:02:14.740 --> 00:02:15.630
Political repression,

00:02:15.630 --> 00:02:17.100
and the seizure of private property

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:18.610
under Castro's rule drove Cubans

00:02:18.610 --> 00:02:19.880
to flee the island nation,

00:02:19.880 --> 00:02:21.710
and it was immigrants
who transformed the city

00:02:21.710 --> 00:02:22.990
from a sleepy backwater

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:24.440
into a Latin American Mecca.

00:02:24.440 --> 00:02:25.940
Florida's population is climbing

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:27.730
because of its business friendly climate.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:29.550
The state is second only to New Hampshire

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:32.380
in the Fraser Institute's
state ranking of economic view.

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.700
The U.S. ranked sixth in
the global rankings,

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:37.880
which explains why Latin American
migrants continue to come.

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:40.760
Venezuela, whose citizens have
escaped to Miami en mass

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.003
in recent years, is dead last, at 162.

00:02:45.540 --> 00:02:46.920
Miami is my home.

00:02:46.920 --> 00:02:49.240
Miami is the place where I feel happy.

00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:51.300
My friends are here. I married here.

00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:54.551
My parents live here too.
I went to school here.

00:02:54.551 --> 00:02:56.910
Caesar Grajales arrived
in Miami from Columbia

00:02:56.910 --> 00:02:59.550
when he was 19 with 80 dollars in his pocket.

00:02:59.550 --> 00:03:01.990
He says that his family fled
Columbia in the nineties,

00:03:01.990 --> 00:03:03.800
because they feared for their lives.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:04.920
At the end of the nineties,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:06.660
that was the peak of the violence,

00:03:06.660 --> 00:03:11.090
with the cartels and guerrillas
that propel

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:15.350
the desire for many people
to just leave the country.

00:03:15.350 --> 00:03:17.640
Columbia not only lacks
a strong legal system,

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:18.630
and rule of law,

00:03:18.630 --> 00:03:21.140
ranking 108th on the Fraser Institute's

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:22.860
Freedom Index in that category,

00:03:22.860 --> 00:03:26.158
it also ranks 93rd on the quality of its currency,

00:03:26.158 --> 00:03:28.072
109th on tariffs,

00:03:28.072 --> 00:03:31.003
and 69th on the size of
its regulatory states.

00:03:31.003 --> 00:03:38.223
So do you think there's any sort of parallel between Latin-Americans escaping their countries

00:03:38.223 --> 00:03:40.588
through to failed
policies to come to Miami,

00:03:40.588 --> 00:03:43.906
and people leaving Silicon
Valley to come to Miami?

00:03:43.906 --> 00:03:46.354
Definitely, people are coming to Florida because

00:03:46.354 --> 00:03:52.442
here, with the decisions that we have been taking lately, and at the state level,

00:03:52.442 --> 00:03:56.551
have proven better than the decisions that we took in other states.

00:03:56.551 --> 00:03:58.597
So definitely there is a connection there.

00:03:58.597 --> 00:04:02.522
California, and the Bay Area in particular, in contrast are case study

00:04:02.522 --> 00:04:05.000
of the failure of big government liberalism.

00:04:05.000 --> 00:04:07.882
I just don't understand how you create success,

00:04:07.882 --> 00:04:10.107
if you push out people that
are creating high-paying jobs.

00:04:10.107 --> 00:04:14.104
That model happened in
Cuba, under communism,

00:04:14.104 --> 00:04:18.902
and the only equality that is created is equal misery for all of its residents.

00:04:18.902 --> 00:04:20.836
That's part of our DNA.
It's part of who we are.

00:04:20.836 --> 00:04:24.133
We believe in ourselves, we invest in ourselves, and we want government 

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.527
to take as least of our money as possible,

00:04:26.527 --> 00:04:28.197
and let us be successful on our own.

00:04:28.197 --> 00:04:32.157
But Mayor Suarez is more a marketing genius  than principled free marketeer,

00:04:32.157 --> 00:04:35.607
and there's always a risk that over time the city government in Miami will come

00:04:35.607 --> 00:04:38.154
to more closely resemble San Francisco's.

00:04:38.154 --> 00:04:43.695
It's becoming more and more difficult for the market to provide the kind of affordable housing that is needed.

00:04:43.695 --> 00:04:47.877
Suarez recently oversaw the borrowing of 100 billion dollars for affordable housing.

00:04:47.877 --> 00:04:50.433
San Francisco, however,
borrowed 300 billion.

00:04:50.433 --> 00:04:54.121
Both cities would better serve their poorest residents by slashing red tape,

00:04:54.121 --> 00:04:57.881
so that the private sector could provide affordable housing, more effectively.

00:04:57.881 --> 00:05:02.773
Mayor Suarez told Axios that he wants to triple the size  of a city program that gives companies financial incentives

00:05:02.773 --> 00:05:04.317
to move to downtown Miami.

00:05:04.317 --> 00:05:06.611
Confusing capitalism with cronyism,

00:05:06.611 --> 00:05:11.062
Suarez told Reason that he considers government handout schemes to be a form of market competition.

00:05:11.062 --> 00:05:15.400
Unfortunately, what happens is other areas subsidize, and so we have to compete with those areas.

00:05:15.400 --> 00:05:19.432
So we want to become competitive, and that's part of being in a capitalist society, is competing.

00:05:19.432 --> 00:05:21.117
We want Miami to be
the capital of capital,

00:05:21.117 --> 00:05:25.608
from New York, and from Los Angeles, and Silicon Valley, and San Francisco.

00:05:25.608 --> 00:05:29.787
And that confluence— we've never seen merge anywhere
in the history of humanity.

00:05:29.787 --> 00:05:31.556
It's not just for the good of Miami.

00:05:31.556 --> 00:05:34.166
Suarez, who's rumored to
have gubernatorial ambitions,

00:05:34.166 --> 00:05:38.438
knows that billionaire VCs are likely to become future donors to his political campaigns.

00:05:38.438 --> 00:05:42.832
In January, venture capitalist, and former Facebook Executive
Chamath Palihapitiya

00:05:42.832 --> 00:05:46.091
cut Suarez a quarter million dollar check for his reelection campaign.

00:05:46.091 --> 00:05:49.469
For Miami, more important than
becoming home to a confluence of capital,

00:05:49.469 --> 00:05:53.388
is that it continues to be the center of a confluence of immigrants from Latin America,

00:05:53.388 --> 00:05:56.506
who have a bigger role to play if the city is to succeed
as a new tech hub.

00:06:09.452 --> 00:06:12.062
Boris Elneser Montiel
is a Venezuela immigrant

00:06:12.062 --> 00:06:14.102
with a profitable startup
called, Ticket Plate,

00:06:14.102 --> 00:06:17.455
which sells online passes
to live concerts streaming on the internet.

00:06:17.455 --> 00:06:21.910
His wife Veronica Ruíz del Vizo, runs her own successful
digital marketing firm.

00:06:58.622 --> 00:07:00.951
Though he doesn't have
relationships with any

00:07:00.951 --> 00:07:03.101
of the VCs major Suarez has his sights on,

00:07:03.101 --> 00:07:04.721
he's well connected within Venezuela,

00:07:04.721 --> 00:07:06.571
which is an important
factor in his success.

00:07:06.571 --> 00:07:08.621
His website was coded back in Venezuela,

00:07:08.621 --> 00:07:11.131
And Elneser's customer
support team is located there.

00:07:11.131 --> 00:07:12.471
He estimates that for the cost

00:07:12.471 --> 00:07:15.361
of one staffer in Miami, he
can hire six in Venezuela.

00:07:44.014 --> 00:07:46.048
Ruíz del Vizo says that for Miami to become more

00:07:46.048 --> 00:07:48.694
of a tech and business hub,
it needs to develop networks

00:07:48.694 --> 00:07:50.807
that better serve
immigrants and entrepreneurs.

00:08:20.162 --> 00:08:23.739
Elesner and Ruíz del Vizo didn't come here primarily to grow their businesses.

00:08:23.739 --> 00:08:26.169
They left Venezuela for the
same reason that Cesar Grajales’

00:08:26.169 --> 00:08:29.449
family fled Columbia: for
stability and the rule of law.

00:09:34.351 --> 00:09:35.767
I think there's a lot of people

00:09:35.767 --> 00:09:37.647
in Miami that are from a
variety of different backgrounds

00:09:37.647 --> 00:09:39.347
but a lot of them are from
current communist countries

00:09:39.347 --> 00:09:42.777
whether they've been in
Venezuela, Nicaraguan, Cuban

00:09:42.777 --> 00:09:44.577
or have been affected by it.

00:09:44.577 --> 00:09:45.587
So yeah, I think

00:09:45.587 --> 00:09:48.067
without a doubt that
there's a pervasive sort

00:09:48.067 --> 00:09:51.582
of a pro capitalistic
bent and mentality here.

00:09:51.582 --> 00:09:52.957
And that's part of what has appealed

00:09:52.957 --> 00:09:57.797
to VCs like PayPal, co-founder Peter Thiel and Keith Rabois,
conservative nonconformists

00:09:57.797 --> 00:10:00.497
who had enough of San
Francisco's anti-business animus

00:10:00.497 --> 00:10:01.996
and woke monoculture.

00:10:01.996 --> 00:10:04.107
I don't see the woke thing
ever taking hold on Miami.

00:10:04.107 --> 00:10:06.083
If you go out and you see people

00:10:06.083 --> 00:10:08.139
half naked and having fun,
you're kind of confronted

00:10:08.139 --> 00:10:11.899
with physical reality in a way
that we'll never—

00:10:11.899 --> 00:10:14.544
woke ideas won't ever translate here,
it's not even translatable.

00:10:14.544 --> 00:10:15.660
That's just no concept of it.

00:10:15.660 --> 00:10:17.899
Alex Perez is a Miami native, a fiction writer

00:10:17.899 --> 00:10:20.479
and a cultural commentator, who
writes about how the outlook

00:10:20.479 --> 00:10:22.549
of the American cognoscenti doesn't translate

00:10:22.549 --> 00:10:23.969
for Miami Latinos,

00:10:23.969 --> 00:10:26.379
especially when it comes
to race and politics.

00:10:26.379 --> 00:10:28.979
All over, there isn't—with the Hispanic people—

00:10:28.979 --> 00:10:30.719
there isn't an affinity with wokeness.

00:10:30.719 --> 00:10:34.079
It's not like—if you were going to my uncle

00:10:34.079 --> 00:10:37.919
and tell him what that is he'd be like, this guy's pulling my leg.

00:10:37.919 --> 00:10:42.465
If I call my dad LatinX he'd try to fight me and be like, what? Que eso?

00:10:42.465 --> 00:10:48.285
So I specifically, the way I see it, like when I write these pieces people are like, nah, he's lying.

00:10:48.285 --> 00:10:55.000
I'm like, no, it's just, I tell people that I'm a writer, a cultural critic and they're like, man, what a sad man.

00:10:55.000 --> 00:10:56.510
What is that? A cultural critic?

00:10:56.510 --> 00:10:58.100
And people think that's like

00:10:58.100 --> 00:10:59.370
speaking of ill of Miami, I said, no.

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:02.608
Like it's just not things
that are really relevant here.

00:11:02.608 --> 00:11:05.374
One critic of cancel culture who recently bought a home in Miami 

00:11:05.374 --> 00:11:07.853
is Barstool Founder Dave Portnoy.

00:11:07.853 --> 00:11:11.260
"You'd have to be a moron to pay New York taxes," he
told the Miami Herald.

00:11:11.260 --> 00:11:13.590
But Portnoy isn't moving
his company quite yet

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:15.860
because he doesn't think his
employees could handle it.

00:11:15.860 --> 00:11:17.640
"I don't know if some of
us have the willpower,"

00:11:17.640 --> 00:11:18.820
Portland told the Herald,

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:21.070
"I don't know what half
the people in Miami do."

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:22.950
"It seems like if you have
to be at work the next day

00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:26.050
after a night out. We do.
We're still normal guys built

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:28.240
for cubes, working from nine to five.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:31.110
And if you've been up till
5:00 AM, that's hard to do."

00:11:31.110 --> 00:11:35.970
That's probably true. I mean, Miami is fast paced, but
also very slow paced.

00:11:35.970 --> 00:11:38.110
Like people are fast
paced, you know, to go out.

00:11:38.110 --> 00:11:39.900
But then you got to meet somebody, 

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:43.210
they might not show up or, you go to a restaurant

00:11:43.210 --> 00:11:45.930
they might speak Spanish
and that'll be fun,

00:11:45.930 --> 00:11:47.720
maybe from a month,
somebody speaks Spanish.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:50.490
"That's funny, how cute." And
then let's see, six months in..

00:11:50.490 --> 00:11:51.410
Do you think, for example

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:52.630
there might be a culture clash

00:11:52.630 --> 00:11:57.924
between Silicon Valley culture, I don't know maybe nerdy engineer culture

00:11:57.924 --> 00:12:00.000
versus that Miami party culture?

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:02.950
What I'm really curious about
Mayor Suarez's pitch is that

00:12:02.950 --> 00:12:06.035
it'll probably work the first
four or five, six months.

00:12:06.035 --> 00:12:09.714
We're in March, April, weather is great. Summer's not here yet, hurricane's aren't coming yet. 

00:12:09.714 --> 00:12:13.040
But I'd be curious, in four or five or six
months, how the tech bros

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:15.123
that are coming into town are going to deal with the culture. 

00:12:15.123 --> 00:12:19.123
Because it's fun for three or four days, a weekend,

00:12:19.123 --> 00:12:23.223
but there is a a wonky-ish nerdish element

00:12:23.223 --> 00:12:26.703
to that culture that I'm not
sure can translate to Miami.

00:12:26.703 --> 00:12:32.613
I think some will love it, but I think those are the people that maybe were from Miami in a spiritual way.

00:12:32.613 --> 00:12:35.233
Miami will only become a new
and improved Silicon Valley

00:12:35.233 --> 00:12:36.893
if it can recreate the tech industry

00:12:36.893 --> 00:12:39.663
without the left-wing nimbyism that made it impossible

00:12:39.663 --> 00:12:42.123
for anyone except the ultra
wealthy to find a place to live

00:12:42.123 --> 00:12:47.363
in San Francisco, or the wokeism that's infected the ranks of Google and other tech giants.

00:12:47.363 --> 00:12:51.603
Miami's best defense is
a cultural predisposition of immigrants from countries

00:12:51.603 --> 00:12:53.743
like Cuba, Venezuela, and Columbia,

00:12:53.743 --> 00:12:57.153
who understand best of all the corrosive
impact of big government.

00:12:57.153 --> 00:13:00.473
We have to create ambiance
for the businesses

00:13:00.473 --> 00:13:06.143
but it doesn't mean intervention, doesn't mean using the taxpayer's money for corporate welfare.

00:13:06.143 --> 00:13:11.861
So that's a fine line of saying to the businesses and big companies, you know what?

00:13:11.861 --> 00:13:13.086
Relocate to my state.

00:13:13.086 --> 00:13:15.716
We have good economic rules here.

00:13:15.716 --> 00:13:17.586
That's a fine line between saying that,

00:13:17.586 --> 00:13:19.246
and then another saying, come here

00:13:19.246 --> 00:13:23.862
because we're going to build X location for you to open your business.

00:13:23.862 --> 00:13:26.647
That's corporate welfare,
that's using taxpayers' money

00:13:26.647 --> 00:13:28.147
and I don't think that's fair.

